@page "/knowledge-base/state"

<PageTitle>State in the knowledge base — Backlog UI Storybook</PageTitle>

<StoryPage Title="State"
           Summary="A subpage of Knowledge base: status is the one field every block must carry, and what it is allowed to say depends on which folder the block came from. KnowledgeStatus holds the five vocabularies, KnowledgeStatusTone is the one visual scale underneath them, and KnowledgeFolder is what connects a document to its own. Adding state support to a component is one parameter — the folder — and everything on this page follows from it.">

    <Alert Message="@FolderNotice" Role="note" CssClass="sb-callout" />

    <Story Name="Five vocabularies, one scale, and both forms of a status"
           Description="Nobody agreed on one set of words, and they were right not to. `.tech` runs a tech-radar ladder, `.backlog` tracks a task through to done, `.arc42` and `.domain` describe a standing structure that is never finished, and `.design` has no proposed stage at all because a design is either being drawn or it is in force. Renaming any of them to suit a stylesheet would make the folder read wrong to the people who write it.

The tone is the shared axis underneath, and it is the columns here: how far along a thing is, and whether it wants attention. Read a row and you have one folder's whole vocabulary; read a column and you have the words five folders spell differently and mean the same by. Two rows are identical because two folders share a list — architecture and domain knowledge both describe something standing rather than something in flight, which is why neither has a `done` — and every empty cell is a stage that folder simply does not have. It also explains why `.tech` reads out of ladder order: a candidate is named but unproven and a trial is being run, so the trial is the more tentative of the two.

Both forms of each status sit in the cell side by side: the read-only KnowledgeStatusPill, and the StatusSelector KnowledgeMetaView draws once the folder names a vocabulary to offer. They are one fact about one chapter, so they are meant to be indistinguishable but for the caret — same colour, same shape, same size, same weight. They can be, because they are the same component underneath: both are the application's status badge, and both ask one builder which of its states this folder's word means. Any cell where the two differ is a bug.

Nothing here is a scale of its own. The tone is the axis — it is the columns — and each tone maps onto a `badge--status-*` modifier the stylesheet already defines for backlog entries: Provisional onto `draft`, Planned onto `ready`, Active onto `active`, Complete onto `done`, Attention onto `blocked`, Retired onto `archived`. So `adopted` in `.tech` is painted by the very rule that paints an entry somebody is working on, and a reader who has learned one folder's colours has learned the whole product's.

Reusing a scale means taking its granularity too. The application badge has one alarm colour, so `.tech`'s `hold` and `.backlog`'s `blocked` come out the same red even though one is a deliberate pause and the other is a problem. That is a distinction this palette does not draw, and it is not worth inventing a seventh colour for; the word inside the badge is what separates them."
           Code="@ValuesCode">
        <div class="sb-table-scroll" data-testid="knowledge-state-matrix">
            <table class="sb-table">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="col">folder</th>
                        @foreach (var tone in Tones)
                        {
                            <th scope="col">
                                <div class="sb-stack">
                                    <span>@tone.Name</span>
                                    <span class="sb-state">@tone.Meaning</span>
                                </div>
                            </th>
                        }
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    @foreach (var folder in Folders)
                    {
                        <tr data-testid="@($"knowledge-state-matrix-{Slug(folder)}")">
                            <th scope="row">@FolderName(folder)</th>
                            @foreach (var tone in Tones)
                            {
                                <td>
                                    @foreach (var cell in CellsFor(folder, tone.Tone))
                                    {
                                        <div class="sb-row" data-testid="@($"knowledge-state-cell-{Slug(folder)}-{cell.Value}")">
                                            <KnowledgeStatusPill Status="@cell.Value"
                                                                 Folder="@folder"
                                                                 TestId="@($"knowledge-state-pill-{Slug(folder)}-{cell.Value}")" />
                                            <KnowledgeMetaView Metadata="@cell.Metadata"
                                                               Folder="@folder"
                                                               AriaLabel="@($"{FolderName(folder)} status")"
                                                               TestId="@($"knowledge-state-select-{Slug(folder)}-{cell.Value}")" />
                                        </div>
                                    }
                                </td>
                            }
                        </tr>
                    }
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </Story>

    <Story Name="The same word, two verdicts, because the folder differs"
           Description="`active` is a perfectly good status in `.design` and is not a status at all in `.tech`, where the equivalent rung is called `adopted`. Both statements are below, and the only thing that separates them is the folder parameter. This is why tone is folder-gated rather than guessed from the word: two folders spell different meanings with the same spelling, and one of them spells the same meaning with a different word, so a guess made without the folder would be wrong about half the time.

The folder decides the shape as well, so read the four cells in two pairs. The first two are selects: the folder is known and the status is one of its values, so every state the control can show is a state that file is allowed to say — and `.design` offers three words where `.tech` offers five, which is the vocabularies being different made concrete. The last two are read-only and must be. `active` is not on `.tech`'s list, and a select with no option for the value it was handed would show `candidate` instead and report a state nobody wrote; the fourth cell has no list at all.

The last cell is also the answer this component gave before any of this existed: the flat mono capsule, its literal `knowledge-status--active` class, no tone and no verdict. Not knowing is a real state and it renders as one.

It is deliberately the only capsule left on the page. That cell is MetadataBadge's two-parameter branch, which is frozen — those callers are entitled to the markup they were written against, down to the attribute order — whereas the typed record's folder-blind form is now the plain status badge with no state modifier. So the two folder-blind answers on this page look different from each other, and that is the freeze showing rather than a bug. Compare it with the folder-blind record in the story below.

Every cell carries the one-line block it was read from, and the badge beside it is handed the status out of that same line rather than a second copy of the word."
           Code="@FolderCode">
        <dl class="sb-grid" data-testid="knowledge-state-same-word">
            @foreach (var verdict in Verdicts)
            {
                <div class="sb-stack">
                    <dt class="sb-label">@verdict.Label</dt>
                    <dd class="sb-stack">
                        <pre class="sb-story__code" data-testid="@($"{verdict.TestId}-source")"><code>@verdict.Block</code></pre>
                        <MetadataBadge Status="@verdict.Status"
                                       Folder="@verdict.Folder"
                                       AriaLabel="@verdict.AriaLabel"
                                       TestId="@verdict.TestId" />
                        <p class="sb-state">@verdict.Reading</p>
                    </dd>
                </div>
            }
        </dl>
    </Story>

    <Story Name="A status nobody recognises is flagged, not quietly styled"
           Description="`activ` is one keystroke away from a value `.design` defines, and that is what makes it dangerous: it looks like a status, it sorts near one, and a viewer that painted it like every other unknown word would let it sit in the file indefinitely. Here it takes a surface that says look at this, and its title names the values that were expected — naming them is the whole point of knowing the vocabulary in the first place.

It stays the same badge as every other status on this page — same box, same size — and wears `archived`, the one modifier that spends no colour: a transparent surface inside a border. This rule adds only the two things that say nobody wrote this. The border goes dashed, and the word comes back to full strength from the muted ink `archived` reads its own values in. The alarming surface was tried and given up: it is the same red `blocked` wears, so a typo and a genuinely blocked chapter could not be told apart at a glance. Quiet and provisional is the truthful reading of a word nobody recognises, and the urgency lives in the title. It is also a badge rather than a select, necessarily: a select could only offer values the folder defines, so putting this one in it would either drop the typo silently or stop flagging it.

An unrecognised status is never an exception and never suppresses the block. What a file says is not something a viewer gets to refuse; it is something a viewer can make visible."
           Code="@UnrecognisedCode">
        <div class="sb-columns" data-testid="knowledge-state-unrecognised">
            <div class="sb-stack">
                <p class="sb-label">The block, as written</p>
                <pre class="sb-story__code" data-testid="knowledge-state-unrecognised-source"><code>@TypoBlock</code></pre>
                <p class="sb-state" data-testid="knowledge-state-unrecognised-check">@TypoSummary</p>
            </div>
            <div class="sb-stack">
                <p class="sb-label">Rendered as a .design chapter</p>
                <KnowledgeMetaView Metadata="@TypoMetadata"
                                   Folder="KnowledgeFolder.Design"
                                   TestId="knowledge-state-unrecognised-view" />
                <p class="sb-label">The same block with no folder given</p>
                <KnowledgeMetaView Metadata="@TypoMetadata"
                                   TestId="knowledge-state-unrecognised-blind" />
            </div>
        </div>
    </Story>

    <Story Name="Adding state support to a component"
           Description="One parameter. Everything on this page is what a component gets for passing a folder down to the badge or the metadata view — the vocabulary, the tone, the flag on a value that is in neither, and the status control, which is the vocabulary offered rather than only checked. That control keeps the reader's choice by itself, so it is honest with nothing wired to it; a host that wants the change to outlive the page adds OnStatusChanged and writes it somewhere.

Derive the folder rather than asking for it. It is always the first segment of a repository-relative path, so KnowledgeFolders.FromPath answers from the address a document already carries, and a reference that was parsed already has it on Folder. A component that took the folder as a hand-set enum would eventually be told the wrong one. Leaving it out is a supported answer, not a missing one: a surface showing metadata from somewhere other than a knowledge folder passes nothing and gets exactly what it got before.

IsKnown and Tone are the same two questions the badge asks itself, exposed for callers that need the answer before they render anything — an editor offering the values a folder allows, a save path refusing to write a word the folder has never defined, a check that walks the repository. IsKnown is trimmed and case-insensitive, because a stray capital is a spelling of the same status rather than a different one, and it answers false for every status when the folder is unknown: false there means not verifiable rather than wrong. The line under the record below is those calls, run."
           Code="@WiringCode">
        <div class="sb-stack" data-testid="knowledge-state-wiring">
            <p class="sb-label">The path, and the block read out of it</p>
            <pre class="sb-story__code" data-testid="knowledge-state-wiring-source"><code>@WiringSource</code></pre>
            <KnowledgeMetaView Metadata="@BacklogMetadata"
                               Folder="@KnowledgeFolders.FromPath(BacklogPath)"
                               TestId="knowledge-state-wiring-view" />
            <p class="sb-state" data-testid="knowledge-state-frompath">@FromPathSummary</p>
            <p class="sb-state" data-testid="knowledge-state-checks">@CheckSummary</p>
        </div>
    </Story>

</StoryPage>

@code {
    private const string FolderNotice =
        "Every behaviour on this page is gated on the folder. With no folder given, the status is still "
        + "shown and simply not judged — no tone, no flag, no control, and no state modifier on the "
        + "badge, which leaves the plain one that says nothing. MetadataBadge's own two-parameter form "
        + "is untouched and still renders exactly the markup it always did.";

    /// <summary>One column of the matrix: a tone, and what it means.</summary>
    private sealed record ToneColumn(KnowledgeStatusTone Tone, string Meaning)
    {
        public string Name => Tone.ToString();
    }

    /// <summary>One status of one folder, with the block it is drawn from held
    /// once. Parsed here rather than in the loop so the record handed to the view
    /// is the same object on every render — a fresh one each time would reseed the
    /// select and undo a reader mid-click.</summary>
    private sealed record StatusCell(KnowledgeFolder Folder, string Value, KnowledgeStatusTone Tone, KnowledgeMetadata Metadata);

    /// <summary>One cell of the same-word story. The status is held once and read
    /// twice — as the block on screen and as the badge's parameter — so the source
    /// shown is provably the source being rendered.</summary>
    private sealed record Verdict(string Label, KnowledgeFolder Folder, string Status, string Reading, string TestId)
    {
        public string Block => $"status: {Status}";

        // "no folder status" is what FolderName would give the last cell, and an
        // accessible name is read aloud rather than skimmed.
        public string AriaLabel => Folder is KnowledgeFolder.Unknown ? "Status" : $"{FolderName(Folder)} status";
    }

    private static readonly IReadOnlyList<KnowledgeFolder> Folders =
    [
        KnowledgeFolder.Arc42,
        KnowledgeFolder.Domain,
        KnowledgeFolder.Backlog,
        KnowledgeFolder.Tech,
        KnowledgeFolder.Design
    ];

    private static readonly IReadOnlyList<ToneColumn> Tones =
    [
        new(KnowledgeStatusTone.Provisional, "written down, not agreed"),
        new(KnowledgeStatusTone.Planned, "agreed and waiting"),
        new(KnowledgeStatusTone.Active, "live and current"),
        new(KnowledgeStatusTone.Complete, "finished"),
        new(KnowledgeStatusTone.Attention, "stuck, or being avoided"),
        new(KnowledgeStatusTone.Retired, "kept for history only")
    ];

    /// <summary>Every value of every folder, read out of KnowledgeStatus rather
    /// than restated here, so the matrix cannot disagree with the component
    /// beside it. The pill and the select in a cell are both drawn from this one
    /// entry, which is what makes them a comparison rather than two claims.</summary>
    private static readonly IReadOnlyList<StatusCell> Cells =
    [
        .. Folders.SelectMany(folder => KnowledgeStatus.Values(folder).Select(value =>
            new StatusCell(
                folder,
                value,
                KnowledgeStatus.Tone(folder, value),
                KnowledgeMeta.Parse($"status: {value}"))))
    ];

    private const string TypoBlock = """
        status: activ
        related: [".design/design-principles.md"]
        """;

    private static readonly KnowledgeMetadata TypoMetadata = KnowledgeMeta.Parse(TypoBlock);

    private const string BacklogPath = ".backlog/domain-backlog.md";

    private const string BacklogBlock = """
        status: in-progress
        implements: [.domain/backlog/features.md#feature-roadmap-planning]
        """;

    private static readonly KnowledgeMetadata BacklogMetadata = KnowledgeMeta.Parse(BacklogBlock);

    /// <summary>The four cells of the same-word story, in the order they read: two
    /// folders that define the word, one that does not, and no folder at all.
    /// The last one keeps MetadataBadge's two-parameter branch, which is the
    /// markup this claim is about.</summary>
    private static readonly IReadOnlyList<Verdict> Verdicts =
    [
        new(".design — active is in force", KnowledgeFolder.Design, "active",
            "Known, tone Active.", "knowledge-state-active-design"),

        new(".tech — the same rung is called adopted", KnowledgeFolder.Tech, "adopted",
            "Known, tone Active — a different word for the same place on the scale.",
            "knowledge-state-adopted-tech"),

        new(".tech — active, which .tech does not define", KnowledgeFolder.Tech, "active",
            "Flagged. The same word that was fine one cell to the left.",
            "knowledge-state-active-tech"),

        new("No folder — no opinion", KnowledgeFolder.Unknown, "active",
            "The markup this component has always rendered.", "knowledge-state-active-unknown")
    ];

    /// <summary>The values of one folder that land on one tone — nearly always
    /// one, occasionally none, which is the empty cell.</summary>
    private static IEnumerable<StatusCell> CellsFor(KnowledgeFolder folder, KnowledgeStatusTone tone) =>
        Cells.Where(cell => cell.Folder == folder && cell.Tone == tone);

    /// <summary>The path above the block it holds, because the folder is read off
    /// the path and the block alone would not show where that came from.</summary>
    private static string WiringSource => $"{BacklogPath}\n\n{BacklogBlock}";

    private static string TypoSummary =>
        $"IsKnown(.design, \"activ\") is {KnowledgeStatus.IsKnown(KnowledgeFolder.Design, "activ")}. "
        + $"Expected one of: {string.Join(", ", KnowledgeStatus.Values(KnowledgeFolder.Design))}.";

    private static string FromPathSummary =>
        $"KnowledgeFolders.FromPath(\"{BacklogPath}\") is "
        + $"{KnowledgeFolders.FromPath(BacklogPath)}, so `in-progress` is read against that folder.";

    /// <summary>The two API calls, run rather than tabulated. Each answer is the
    /// call itself evaluated, so a row cannot end up asking one question and
    /// printing another.</summary>
    private static string CheckSummary =>
        $"IsKnown(.backlog, \"in-progress\") is {KnowledgeStatus.IsKnown(KnowledgeFolder.Backlog, "in-progress")}; "
        + $"IsKnown(.backlog, \"  In-Progress \") is {KnowledgeStatus.IsKnown(KnowledgeFolder.Backlog, "  In-Progress ")} — "
        + "trimmed and case-insensitive; "
        + $"IsKnown(.backlog, \"adopted\") is {KnowledgeStatus.IsKnown(KnowledgeFolder.Backlog, "adopted")} — "
        + "a real status, in another folder; "
        + $"IsKnown(no folder, \"done\") is {KnowledgeStatus.IsKnown(KnowledgeFolder.Unknown, "done")} — "
        + "no vocabulary to check against; "
        + $"Tone(no folder, \"active\") is {KnowledgeStatus.Tone(KnowledgeFolder.Unknown, "active")}.";

    private static string Slug(KnowledgeFolder folder) => folder.ToString().ToLowerInvariant();

    /// <summary>The folder as it is spelt on disk, which is what an author
    /// recognises — the enum name is an implementation detail.</summary>
    private static string FolderName(KnowledgeFolder folder) => folder switch
    {
        KnowledgeFolder.Arc42 => ".arc42",
        KnowledgeFolder.Domain => ".domain",
        KnowledgeFolder.Backlog => ".backlog",
        KnowledgeFolder.Tech => ".tech",
        KnowledgeFolder.Design => ".design",
        _ => "no folder"
    };

    private const string ValuesCode = """
        @* The folder's own list, in the order its instructions give. An editor
           offering a status should offer these and nothing else. *@
        foreach (var value in KnowledgeStatus.Values(KnowledgeFolder.Tech))
        {
            // candidate, trial, adopted, hold, retired
        }

        @* And the axis underneath the five vocabularies, which is what lets one
           colour mean the same thing in every folder. *@
        KnowledgeStatusTone tone = KnowledgeStatus.Tone(KnowledgeFolder.Tech, "adopted"); // Active
        """;

    private const string FolderCode = """
        @* One parameter. The vocabulary, the tone and the flag all follow from
           it, and leaving it off is a supported answer rather than a missing one. *@
        <MetadataBadge Metadata="@metadata" Folder="KnowledgeFolder.Design" />

        <KnowledgeMetaView Metadata="@metadata" Folder="KnowledgeFolder.Tech" />

        @* Passing nothing keeps the flat pill this component always drew. *@
        <MetadataBadge Status="@status" Related="@relatedIds" />
        """;

    private const string UnrecognisedCode = """
        @* What the badge does with a value the folder does not define. It is
           answered before any tone is consulted, and the answer is `archived` —
           the badge that spends no colour — plus the flag, which dashes the
           border and brings the word back to full strength. The title names what
           was expected. *@
        <span class="badge badge--status badge--status-archived knowledge-status--unrecognised"
              title="Unexpected status. Expected one of: draft, active, deprecated.">activ</span>
        """;

    private const string WiringCode = """
        @* Derive the folder from the path rather than asking a caller for it —
           it is always the first segment, and a parsed reference already carries
           it on Folder. *@
        <KnowledgeMetaView Metadata="@metadata"
                           Folder="@KnowledgeFolders.FromPath(documentPath)" />

        @* The same on a whole document, reaching every block in the body. *@
        <MarkdownView Blocks="@blocks"
                      RenderKnowledgeMetadata="true"
                      KnowledgeFolder="@KnowledgeFolders.FromPath(documentPath)" />

        @* The same question the badge asks itself, for a caller that needs the
           answer before it renders anything. *@
        if (!KnowledgeStatus.IsKnown(folder, status))
        {
            var expected = string.Join(", ", KnowledgeStatus.Values(folder));
            problems.Add($"{path}: status '{status}'. Expected one of: {expected}.");
        }
        """;
}
